  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Anjali vs State Of Punjab And Others on 23 October, 2024 Author: Harsimran Singh Sethi Bench: Harsimran Singh Sethi Neutral Citation No:=2024:PHHC:138833 Cwp No.13312 of 2020(O&M) -1- In the High Court of Punjab and Haryana at Chandigarh (108 +233) Cm No.18265-CWP of 2023 in/and Cwp No.13312 of 2020(O&M) Date of Decision: 23.10.2024 Anjali .....Petitioner Versus State of Punjab and others ....Respondents Coram: Hon'Ble Mr. Justice Harsimran Singh Sethi Present: Mr. Vinod Kumar, Advocate for the petitioner. Mr. Arun Gupta, Deputy Advocate General, Punjab. Ms. Gehna Vaishnavi, Advocate for respondent No.3. **** Harsimran Singh Sethi, J. (Oral) Cm No.18265-CWP-2023 Exemption application is allowed as prayed for. Cm No.18272-CWP of",
    "facts": "",
    "arguments": "In the present petition, the grievance being raised by the petitioner is that the petitioner sought to compete for the post of Food Safety 1 of 3 Neutral Citation No:=2024:PHHC:138833 Officer as advertised vide Advertisement No.1 of 2020, copy of which has been appended as Annexure P-1 but the respondents have declared the petitioner ineligible on the ground that the petitioner does not fulfill the qualifications laid down therein so as to claim eligibility. 2. Learned counsel for the petitioner argues that the petitioner has Bachelor of Science (B.Sc.) Degree and while doing the B.Sc., one of the subjects which the petitioner has studied is also Bio-chemistry and, therefore, the petitioner's degree should be treated as a degree in Bio- chemistry. 3. Upon notice of motion, the respondents have filed the reply wherein they have stated that the qualification which the petitioner has is a simple B.Sc. Degree and not a degree in Bio-chemistry, which is entirely a different qualification, hence, the claim of the petitioner that she is eligible, is incorrect."
  },
